The loop gives you two things per iteration: the position and a copy of the element at that position. The copy is the trap — anything you do to it stays done to the copy only.

```go
type Task struct{ Done bool }
tasks := []Task{{false}, {false}}

for _, t := range tasks {
    t.Done = true      // modifies the COPY
}
fmt.Println(tasks[0].Done)  // false — nothing changed
```

The fix is to use the index and write back through it:

```go
for i := range tasks {
    tasks[i].Done = true   // writes through to the slice's storage
}
```

or range over a slice of pointers, where the copy is the pointer — pointing at the same underlying struct, so field writes land.

The `_` in the buggy version is where the tell lives: by discarding the index you lose the only write-back handle. `for i := range s` with `s[i]` is the pattern that can't fail this way — same simplicity, no copy hazard.

Why does range copy the element? Because the loop variable is an ordinary variable, and range's assignment into it is ordinary assignment — for value types (structs, ints, strings), assignment copies. The loop var is written fresh each iteration from the slot, and your body writes to that fresh copy, not the slot.

Watch the trap with a struct slice (executed):
```go
type Task struct{ Done bool }
tasks := []Task{{false}, {false}}
for _, t := range tasks {
    t.Done = true
}
fmt.Println(tasks[0].Done) // false — the loop never touched the slice
```
The write landed in t — a stack variable the runtime reuses per iteration — and the slice's backing array never saw it. Contrast the fix, `for i := range tasks { tasks[i].Done = true }`: here there's no element copy at all (you use the index), and the write goes through the slice's storage directly.

The pointer-slice case explains the boundary: `[]*Task` ranges with the copy being the POINTER — 8 bytes, pointing at the same Task. Field writes through the copy land on the shared struct. So 'range copies the element' is precisely true, and for pointers the copy is a reference — the aliasing you want.

Go 1.22 note: the loop variable is now fresh per iteration (the capture-bug fix), but it is STILL a copy of the element for value types — the two changes are orthogonal, and the copy-trap survives.

Three operations, three rules. Update existing values: safe and common — the iteration visits each key once, and writing to a key it has or hasn't reached yet is well-defined.

```go
for k, v := range m {
    m[k] = v * 2     // safe — updating existing entries
}
```

Delete during range: safe — entries deleted before the iterator reaches them may or may not be visited (unspecified, but not a crash), and deletion never invalidates iteration:

```go
for k := range m {
    if expired(k) {
        delete(m, k)   // safe
    }
}
```

Add new keys: unspecified — the iterator may or may not visit entries added during the loop, and it's easy to construct an infinite loop (adding while iterating reaching the new entries, adding again). The GC-safe rule everyone uses: don't insert during range; if you must, collect the new keys first and insert after the loop.

The mental model: range takes a snapshot of the iteration mechanics, not the contents. Mutation of values stays consistent; structural changes (delete) are tolerated with relaxed promises; growth (insert) is where the runtime refuses to promise anything.

The bug every Go codebase of a certain age contains:

```go
// Go 1.21 and earlier
for _, task := range tasks {
    go func() {
        process(task)   // captures the SHARED task variable
    }()
}
// all goroutines likely process the LAST task — by the time they run,
// task has been reassigned to the final iteration's value
```

The cause: pre-1.22, the loop variable was a single memory location reused across iterations. The closure captured that location, not the value — so when goroutines finally executed, they all read whatever the loop left in it: the last value.

The old fixes were workarounds: pass it in (`go func(t Task) { process(t) }(task)`) or shadow it (`task := task` inside the loop).

Go 1.22 changed the semantics: each iteration gets a fresh variable. The closure-capture bug simply disappears on 1.22+ — capturing the loop variable now captures that iteration's value. This is a language change, not a runtime optimization, so the answer must be version-scoped: on 1.22+, the gotcha is history; on older code you still meet it, and the pass-it-in fix is still the right pattern for clarity.

The two keywords cover the single-loop cases:

```go
for _, v := range items {
    if skip(v) {
        continue        // next iteration
    }
    if fatal(v) {
        break           // exit the loop entirely
    }
    process(v)
}
```

The case the keywords don't cover: breaking out of a NESTED loop from the inner body. That's what labels are for — the one labeled-statement use that survives modern Go:

```go
outer:
    for _, row := range matrix {
        for _, cell := range row {
            if cell == target {
                break outer    // exits BOTH loops
            }
        }
    }
```

Note what Go deliberately lacks: labeled continue. goto exists (and is occasionally the clean answer for error-cleanup ladders), but the loop-control set is continue, break, and labeled break — no labeled continue, which the designers considered unnecessary after the labeled-break case.

In idiomatic Go, deep nesting with labels is rare — the culture prefers early returns and small functions, so a labeled break usually signals a loop body that wants to be its own function returning an error/flag. Labels are the honest tool when extraction would be more ceremony; extraction is the default when it reads better.

Labels are the feature people reach for last and should reach for earlier. The semantics: a label marks a statement; `break LABEL` exits the labeled loop regardless of nesting depth. Without it, break binds to the innermost loop only — and the boolean-flag-across-nested-loops pattern is what labels replace:

```go
// the pattern labels replace
found := false
for _, row := range matrix {
    for _, cell := range row {
        if cell == target {
            found = true
            break   // exits inner only — outer keeps scanning
        }
    }
    if found { break }
}
```
versus the labeled form, which says exactly what it means:

```go
usersByCity:  // find the first user in any city matching a prefix
outer:
    for city, users := range directory {
        for _, u := range users {
            if strings.HasPrefix(u.Name, "inv") {
                fmt.Println("found:", city, u.Name)
                break outer  // directory search done — stop scanning everything
            }
        }
    }
```
Why no labeled continue? The Go spec discussions concluded it added a second axis of control flow for a case that extracting a function handles better — continue-to-outer-label is almost always a sign the inner loop wants to BE a function returning 'found it or not.' The tool set (continue, break, labeled break, goto for cleanup ladders) covers the real cases; the missing piece pushes you toward better structure.

goto's one surviving idiomatic use is the same family: error-ladder cleanup in C-era code translated to Go — forward goto a cleanup block, fall-through otherwise. Every other goto in review is a refactor request.
